# A vertical slice

The order is strict: every step builds on the result of the one before.{% if include_codegen %}
While rendering, the generator **imports** the classes a specification refers to, so whatever is
referred to has to exist first.{% endif %}

Before a file of any layer, read that layer's `__init__.py` docstring — what may go there is
their decision.

{% if include_codegen %}## 1. Domain

Entities, value objects and rules. A specification refers to them by a dotted path
(`domain.item.Item`), and that path is imported while the code is generated: entities are
written first.

## 2. The application specification

`{{ project_name }}/application/spec/<file>.yaml`. Its language is in the README next to it.
One file is one resource: its enums, DTOs, use cases and ports.

- the output of a use case must be a DTO of **this same file** (or `none`);
- a port that needs state inside the process (an in-memory store, a pool, a client) declares
  `scope: app` — with the default `request` it is rebuilt on every call and loses its data;
- parameters of an implementation (paths, addresses, timeouts, keys) are `settings: <Name>Settings`.

## 3. The first run of the generator

```bash
uv run python -m tools.codegen
```

DTOs and ports appear (never edit them), and use cases, port implementations and fakes are
appended to the skeletons.

A skeleton is written **once per class**: on a second iteration the generator exits with code 0
and adds nothing inside a class already written. A port added to `ports:` of an existing use case,
and a method added to an existing port, are written by hand — in the use case, in the
implementation and in the fake.

## 4. The bodies

Business logic goes into the domain entity; a use case only orchestrates: load through a port,
call the domain, save through a port. The entity → Output DTO mapping is written by hand.
Port implementations go to `infrastructure/` (or to the module from `implementation:`), fakes to
`tests/fakes/`.

## 5. The router specification

`{{ project_name }}/presentation/fastapi/spec/<file>.yaml`. It refers to the DTOs from step 3 —
which is why it comes now and not earlier.

## 6. The second run of the generator

```bash
uv run python -m tools.codegen
```

Two runs are not belt and braces: on the first one the DTOs the router specification imports do
not exist yet.

## 7. Tests
{% else %}## 1. Domain

Entities, value objects and rules.

## 2. Application

Input and output DTOs (`dto/`), ports (`interfaces/`), the use case (`use_cases/`).

- the input is a dedicated `@dto` per use case, never a primitive and never None;
- a port that needs state inside the process declares `scope = "app"`;
- business logic goes into the domain; a use case only orchestrates.

## 3. Infrastructure

Port implementations. Their parameters are a `BaseSettings` of their own with its own
`env_prefix`, arriving in `__init__`.

{% if include_fastapi %}## 4. Presentation

Request and response schemas, the router and the `exception_handlers` table — the recipe, with
every type spelled out, is in the `presentation/__init__.py` docstring.

## 5. Composition

{% if include_dishka and auto_wire_ports %}Port implementations and their settings need no registration: the provider finds them by scanning the package.
{% else %}Registration of the port implementations and their settings.
{% endif %}The entrypoint is wired by hand: `app.include_router(...)` and `exception_handlers=...` in `composition/api.py`.

## 6. Tests
{% else %}## 4. Composition

{% if include_dishka and auto_wire_ports %}Port implementations and their settings need no registration: the provider finds them by scanning the package.
{% else %}Registration of the port implementations and their settings.
{% endif %}
## 5. Tests
{% endif %}{% endif %}
Which test goes where is in the `tests/__init__.py` docstring. A new test must fail without the
change it covers.

## The check

At the end — the `green` skill: `.agents/skills/green/SKILL.md`.
